.\" diff.1 -- maux's diff(1), in the V7 man(1) shape. An original
.\" page (this project's own text, written against src/bin/diff.s
.\" and src/bin/diffctx.s), so it carries no third-party notice --
.\" MIT like the code, same posture as env.1/man.1/tsort.1.
.TH DIFF 1 "October 8, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
diff \- compare two text files, or two directory trees, line by line
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B diff
[
.B \-rb
] [
.B \-e
|
.B \-u
|
.B \-c
] [
.BI \-U " n"
] [
.BI \-C " n"
]
.I file1 file2
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Diff
reports the lines that must change to turn
.I file1
into
.IR file2 .
The edit script it reports is MINIMAL: both files are read
whole, a longest\-common\-subsequence table is filled over
whole lines, and the script is recovered by backtracking that
table \(em not a heuristic that may report more changes than
are needed.
.PP
With no option the output is the classic ed\-script\-compatible
form:
.RI \(lq 2c2 \(rq
style commands, with the deleted lines marked
.RB \(lq "< " \(rq
and the added lines marked
.RB \(lq "> " \(rq
around a
.RB \(lq \-\-\- \(rq
separator.
.PP
If both operands are directories, the entries of the two are
compared in the byte order of their names (the C locale, so
.RB \(lq Banana \(rq
precedes
.RB \(lq apple \(rq):
a name in only one is reported as
.RB \(lq "Only in \fIdir\fP: \fIname\fP" \(rq,
a pair of plain files is compared, and a pair of directories is
named as
.RB \(lq "Common subdirectories: \fId1\fP and \fId2\fP" \(rq
(or, with
.BR \-r ,
compared in turn).  A name that is a directory in one and a
plain file in the other is reported as
.RB \(lq "File \fId1\fP is a directory while file \fId2\fP is a regular file" \(rq
and counts as a difference.  Each pair of files that differs is
preceded by a line
.RB \(lq "diff \fIoptions d1/x d2/x\fP" \(rq
with the options as they were typed.
If only one operand is a directory, the other is compared with
the file of the same name in it.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-r
Compare directories recursively.
.TP
.B \-b
Treat trailing white space as nothing, and any run of white
space as equal to any other run.
.TP
.B \-e
Write a pure
.BR ed (1)
script \(em commands only, no marked context lines \(em with
the hunks in REVERSE order, so that earlier line numbers are
still valid as
.I ed
applies each edit in turn.
.TP
.B \-u
Write a UNIFIED context diff: two header lines naming the
files, then one
.RB \(lq "@@ \-l,s +l,s @@" \(rq
hunk header per group of changes, followed by the changed and
surrounding lines interleaved, prefixed
.RB \(lq "\ " \(rq
(context),
.RB \(lq \- \(rq
(deleted) and
.RB \(lq + \(rq
(added).  This is the form
.BR patch (1)
prefers.
.TP
.B \-c
Write a CONTEXT diff: two header lines, then per group a
.RB \(lq *************** \(rq
separator, the
.I file1
section (lines prefixed
.RB \(lq "\ \ " \(rq,
.RB \(lq "\- " \(rq
or
.RB \(lq "! " \(rq)
and the
.I file2
section (prefixed
.RB \(lq "\ \ " \(rq,
.RB \(lq "+ " \(rq
or
.RB \(lq "! " \(rq).
A section whose own side has no changed line at all is written
as its header line alone, with no body \(em what every other
.I diff
does for a pure insertion or a pure deletion.
.TP
.BI \-U " n"
.TP
.BI \-C " n"
Select the unified or context form AND set the number of
copied context lines to
.IR n ,
instead of the default 3.  Two groups of changes are printed
as ONE hunk exactly while at most 2\fIn\fR unchanged lines
separate them.
.SH FILES
.br
/usr/man/DIFF  this page
.SH "SEE ALSO"
cmp(1), comm(1), ed(1), patch(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is 0 if the files are identical (and nothing is
written at all), 1 if differences were found, and 2 for a usage
error, a file that cannot be opened, or an input too large for
the tables or the name arena (see BUGS), which is reported as
.RI \(lq "diff: input too large" \(rq
on standard error.  A directory tree is never compared from a
truncated listing: it is refused whole.
.SH BUGS
Capacity is fixed at build time and a file past it is REFUSED,
never silently truncated: 120 lines per file, 6000 bytes of
text across both files, and a comparison table bounded by
(lines1+1)\(mu(lines2+1) \(<= 10000 \(em roughly 99 lines
against 99.
.PP
The
.B \-u
and
.B \-c
header lines carry the pathname alone.  POSIX leaves the
timestamp that usually follows it unspecified, and a ProDOS
file has no meaningful modification time on a machine that
booted without a clock, so none is written.
.BR patch (1)
here reads the name to a tab OR to end of line, so both forms
apply.
.PP
.B \-r
reads each directory whole into a 12000\-byte name arena (17
bytes an entry, both listings of every level still being walked
live in it) and closes it before anything else opens, so the walk
never holds more than the two files being compared.  A tree whose
listings do not fit, one more than 16 directories deep, or one
whose pathname passes the 64 characters ProDOS allows, is
refused with exit status 2.  Each pair of files is still bounded
as above.
.PP
Where the hosts disagree the page follows POSIX: a file
against a directory of the same name is a difference, exit 1
(GNU says 1, BSD says 0).  The same file named twice is
identical and is not opened twice.
